#{[17592186281599 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281599 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163095" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281599 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281599 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=a+b{x}^{2}+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281599 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f950d98b-8d67-594b-b76b-e7454ed0e566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281599 #edb/ref 1074 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =2(1+2{x}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =2(1+2{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(0)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+1{x}^{2}+1{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+1=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281602 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281602 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163102" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281602 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281602 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xy,\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & ∗ \\\\ 2 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186281602 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7c75fe7-9dcd-544f-9c7d-b36cf94f46b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281602 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y & \\frac{x}{y}+x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{y}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}+1 & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n2+(-1)+(x-1)=x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186281603 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281603 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163104" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281603 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281603 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=(x-y)^{3},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & ∗ \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186281603 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b07db089-383d-5673-822f-6a5d7846d04c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281603 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3(x-y)^{2} & -3(x-y)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}6(x-y) & -6(x-y) \\\\ -6(x-y) & 6(x-y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(0,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\nx+6+(y-1)=x+y+5.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186281604 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281604 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163111" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281604 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281604 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y+1),$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=ax-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281604 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46cd9f15-1dee-5aee-b3d9-70903f558ae8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281604 #edb/ref 1074 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{x-y+1}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{x-y+1} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+1\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(1)\\cdot xy+(-1)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1x-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+1xy+(-\\frac{1}{2}){y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281609 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281609 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163143" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281609 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281609 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186281609 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0b608b5-0740-5c5c-a8d8-286478d0cfd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281609 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defened by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{x+y}+2x & \\frac{1}{x+y}+3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}+2 & -\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}} & -\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}+6y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(0,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n1+4+(y-1)+(y-1)+5+(y-1)=3y+7.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186281610 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281610 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281610 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281610 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{3x-y},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & -e\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ -3e & e\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186281610 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "61a1651c-7c64-5ebe-93b5-ec0cfeb0b0a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281610 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defened by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3x-y} & -{e}^{3x-y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3e & -e\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}9{e}^{3x-y} & -3{e}^{3x-y} \\\\ -3{e}^{3x-y} & {e}^{3x-y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}9e & -3e \\\\ -3e & e\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈e+\\begin{bmatrix}3e & -e\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}9e & -3e \\\\ -3e & e\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\ne+3e+(y-2)+(y-2)+9e+(-3e)=2y+10e-4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186281761 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281761 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281761 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08639b07-25fa-583e-a83f-d337dffebecf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281829 #edb/ref 1080 "-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281829 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281829 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f8a1f57-6edf-5d51-9b7c-2d9bbd429811" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281872 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281872 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281872 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "13106fba-e2d8-590f-9a50-2b1d63582d06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281887 #edb/ref 1080 "7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281887 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281887 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "152ed58b-832e-5b30-8ecd-f7c2f04c36a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281934 #edb/ref 1080 "-2{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281934 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281934 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1abdb628-18f3-5897-af88-0cc7b944c171" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282054 #edb/ref 1080 "{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282054 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282054 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2a19cffe-821c-5512-84e1-9848e5f50a0d" #edb/ref 1