In this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-e, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-e & e\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-e+\\begin{bmatrix}-e & e\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & ≈-e+\\begin{bmatrix}-e & e\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0-1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}0-1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0-1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-e+\\begin{bmatrix}-e & e\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-e+2e+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}e \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =e+\\frac{1}{2}(-e+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{e+1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186284523 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284523 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284523 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284523 #edb/ref 1071 "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =0, & \\,{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186284523 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af702ba5-d262-51dd-8530-cde831134efb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284523 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =0, & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈0+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & ≈0+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ -(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186367812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367812 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367812 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367812 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186367817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367812 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8a4e6984-cf2e-55cb-9a10-debfbaccb6f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367813 #edb/ref 1080 "1-3x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367813 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367813 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "40a5e6d9-c4fd-57bb-8147-cf98861b9d12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367814 #edb/ref 1080 "2-x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367814 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367814 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1719c348-9c4f-5f66-9ae0-a4eafaef1b2c" #