is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =0, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈0+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x+1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x+1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x+1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,1) & ≈0+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2+1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2+1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2+1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0+(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}(-4) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186432372 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432372 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163127" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432372 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432372 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3x+2y+{x}^{2}{y}^{3},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+\\begin{bmatrix}5 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186432372 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c474ae22-f722-549c-93e7-7a344f22e1ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432372 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defened by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3+2x{y}^{3} & 2+3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{3} & 6x{y}^{2} \\\\ 6x{y}^{2} & 6{x}^{2}y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈6+\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n6+5+(x-1)+(x-1)+6+(x-1)=3x+14.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186432373 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432373 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164860" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432373 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432373 #edb/ref 1071 "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,-1,-1) & =4, & \\,{f}^{′}(2,-1,-1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(2,-1,-1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(1,0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186432373 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e65d6e32-4824-5250-9bff-fdfee93e977a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432373 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =4, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈4+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y+1 \\\\ z+1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y+1 \\\\ z+1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y+1 \\\\ z+1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,0) & ≈4+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}1-2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4+1+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5+\\frac{1}{2}(-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186432374 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432374 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163109" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432374 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432374 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x+y+1},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=1+ax-y+{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432374 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f2c36de6-e7f2-52de-808d-79c266cdf722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432374 #edb/ref 1074 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+y+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+(-1)\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(2)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+(-1)x-y+{x}^{2}+2xy+1{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-1+2+1=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443613 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186443614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443613 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186443615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443613 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186443616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443613 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186443617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443613 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186443618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443613 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443613 #edb/ref 1045 "Second-Degree Taylor Polynomials of Multivariable Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443613 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186443617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443613 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443613 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443613 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "546808cb-e267-5a30-b354-558dfdac41c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443614 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186443615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443614 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "31443553-9826-5013-9942-cae2c6526a41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443614 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186281609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443615 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186443616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443615 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b9065